{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "UML Sequence Diagrams"}, {"type": "position", "content": 11}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear when drawing a sequence diagram."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common simple incidents at work in a simple spoken or written situation with a supportive interlocutor. Build the macrosystem conceptual model following the customer requirements, through the use and interpretation of collected information, represented in class diagrams, interaction diagrams, and operation contracts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation. Recognizes basic ideas on programming tools, diagrams of cases, and data flow diagrams."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob11re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Search examples of sequence diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Choose one and interpret it."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write your interpretation in a piece of paper."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share with your instructor. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I identify sequence diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I interpret sequence diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain my interpretation of the sequence diagram. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Continuous in Questions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class= \"strong\"> questions in present continuous</span> to ask for information that is set in a specific moment in present."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For making <span class= \"strong\">interrogative</span> statements you need to follow this grammatical structure: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\">Am / Is / Are + Subject + Verb-ing + Complement +? </div></br>e.g.  <span class=\"italic\"> Are you using a UML diagram?</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Besides yes/no questions, you have also the possibility of asking information questions. These kind of questions use a Wh- word in order to center on a specific piece of information, such as <span class= \"strong\">what</span>  (a specific object or piece of information); <span class= \"strong\">when</span>  (time); <span class= \"strong\">how</span>  (manner); <span class= \"strong\">where</span>  (place); <span class= \"strong\">why</span>  (reason); and <span class= \"strong\">which</span>  (choice).  <br><br>  The structure for this kind of questions is very simple: <br><br>  <div class=\"center_text\">Wh- + Verb to be + Subject + Verb \u2013 ing *+ Complement + ? </div>  <br><br> Example: <br> <span class= \"italic\">  <span class= \"strong\"> What</span>  are you planning to do with that information? <br> <span class= \"strong\"> How</span>  are you planning to organize it? </span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Is it describing the architecture or the state of a system?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "How are you representing this process in a diagram?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Subject and object pronouns"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">subject and object pronouns</span> to replace subject or objects within a statement."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use subject pronouns when we want to reduce proper or common nouns repetition within statements. </br> Example:<br> <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">The programmer</span> is drawing the new sequence diagram. </br>         <span class=\"strong\">He</span> (he = the programmer) is drawing the new sequence diagram. </span> </br></br>  You use object pronouns when you want to replace the object or who receives the action. </br> Example:<br><span class=\"italic\"> He is drawing the new <span class=\"strong\">sequence diagram</span></span>.</br>       <span class=\"italic\">  He is drawing <span class=\"strong\">it</span> (the new sequence diagram)</span>. </br> You can always use object pronouns after a preposition e.g. <span class=\"italic\"><span class= \"strong\"> for</span>  him</span>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":8,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Subject Pronoun\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Object Pronoun\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Me\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Him\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Her\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"It\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"It\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Us\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"They\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Them\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "They clutter sequence diagrams, because we need interaction frames for representing them in sequence diagrams."}, {"type": "example", "content": "ATM allows him to withdraw his money."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We're using them to describe how a system works over a period of time. They aren't static, but dynamic."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "method invocation "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "objects that can interact on different diagrams but with the same network"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Are they showing the sequence of method invocations?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "iteration"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "repetition"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The logic of a scenario depends on them, selection \u2018ifs' and iteration \u2018loops'."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "dashed line "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "dashes you use to make a line"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Vertical dashed lines show that time runs from top to bottom."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "loop"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "circuit"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The logic of a scenario depends on them, selection \u2018ifs' and iteration \u2018loops'."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob11aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "interaction frame "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "unit of behavior that represents a notion or an interaction"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They clutter sequence diagrams, because we nee